// REGION 5 — THE PARTIALLY-APPLIED-RETRACTION WINDOW OF DRed.
// HARVESTED FROM THE INTERPRETER BEFORE P5 DELETES IT.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE RECORDS A DIVERGENCE, NOT AN AGREEMENT. It is written to the rule
// that an interpreter answer is checked AGAINST an independently derived closed
// form and never copied FROM it — and here the interpreter LOSES. Three cells
// below assert what the engine does today AND what the semantics say, side by
// side, so the gap cannot vanish when someone repairs one of them.
//
// ── WHY THIS CANNOT BE ASKED AFTER P5 ─────────────────────────────────────
// The static tier's emitted `<rel>_scc0_od` / `_scc0_odp` pair contains NO ERROR
// PATH AT ALL. MEASURED on the emitter, 2026-08-07: `fn emit_scc_od_fns`
// (stdlib/mem/wql/rexpr_walk.logos:8390) declares the return type
// `"Result<(), ElError>"` and the two bodies it emits contain ZERO `Err(`
// constructions — both end in `return Result::Ok(());`. So there is nothing
// static to compare against, and the only artifact in the tree that has ever
// EXECUTED a faulting retraction is `fn dred`
// (stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos:776), which P5 deletes.
//
// ── THE THREE CONTRACTS A FAULTING EPOCH COULD HONOUR, DERIVED ────────────
// Write PRES for the edge set after the batch is applied, and EFC(PRES) for the
// ERROR-FREE CLOSURE: the least fixpoint of the rule over PRES computed with
// CHECKED arithmetic, where a derivation whose running sum overflows contributes
// nothing and blocks its own extensions. (`p.s + e.w` is evaluated left to
// right, so it is the PREFIX sums that must be representable — that is a fact
// about the rule, not about any engine.) Then:
//
//   C1 ATOMIC      — a faulting epoch is a NO-OP: relation AND edge weights are
//                    exactly what they were; `had_error()` reports the fault.
//   C2 BEST-EFFORT — relation == EFC(PRES) exactly; `had_error()` reports that
//                    some derivation was dropped.
//   C3 POISON      — the handle refuses to answer at all until rebuilt.
//
// All three are defensible. What is NOT defensible is a residual that depends on
// WHERE IN THE PHASE SCHEDULE the fault happened to land, because that is not a
// function of the caller's input.
//
// ── WHAT `is_converged()` PROMISES, AND WHY IT MATTERS HERE ───────────────
// `pub fn is_converged` (incr_rec.logos:958) is documented "True iff the
// maintained state is the exact least fixpoint". Under C2 that is still true (of
// the error-free semantics). Under C1 it is FALSE: the relation is the fixpoint
// over the OLD edge set while `self.ewt` already holds PRES — the engine's two
// halves describe different worlds. `fn epoch` sets `self.conv = true`
// unconditionally on the retraction path (incr_rec.logos:582), AFTER `dred`
// has returned, whether or not `dred` aborted. Every cell below asserts
// `is_converged()` by name for that reason.
//
// ── WHAT WAS MEASURED, 2026-08-07, ON THIS TREE ───────────────────────────
// The interpreter honours NONE of C1/C2/C3, and the three cells land on THREE
// DIFFERENT residuals from the same kind of fault:
//
//   E1  fault inside PHASE 1b  → `if chk_bad(cp) { self.math_err = true; return; }`
//       (incr_rec.logos:855) returns BEFORE compact. The relation is the
//       PRE-retraction closure, which is INCOMPARABLE with EFC(PRES) in BOTH
//       directions: it RETAINS facts whose only support was deleted and it
//       LACKS the facts the same batch's insertion made derivable. Meanwhile
//       `self.ewt` already holds PRES. C1 for the relation, PRES for the edges:
//       a hybrid, and `is_converged()` says true.
//   E2  fault inside PHASE 3   → `if chk.failed { self.math_err = true; }`
//       (incr_rec.logos:915) does NOT return; the compacted + partially
//       re-derived rows are COMMITTED. The relation is a strict SUBSET of
//       EFC(PRES), and what it lost is a fact in a DIFFERENT CONNECTED COMPONENT
//       whose own derivation never overflows.
//       ⚠ THE MECHANISM IS NOT THE LOOP HEAD, AND THAT WAS MEASURED, NOT
//       ASSUMED. Removing `if chk_bad(cp) { break; }` from the re-derive loop
//       (incr_rec.logos:~907) changes NOTHING — exit 0, control run 2026-08-07.
//       The loss happens one level down, in `fn qrel_run_body`
//       (stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos:227): `let out = exec_root(…); if
//       chk_bad(chk) { return; }` throws away the ENTIRE round's output table
//       when any one projection cell faulted, correct rows and all, so the
//       fixpoint's next promote sees an empty delta and stops.
//       `is_converged()` says true.
//   E3  the hybrid is ABSORBING. Retracting the very edge that caused the fault
//       does not clear it: phase 1a's `existing-rel ⋈ DE` term re-derives the
//       same overflowing row, `chk_bad` short-circuits phase 1b's loop
//       immediately, and :855 returns before compact again. The relation still
//       holds a fact whose supporting edge no longer exists, and every further
//       retraction that touches it re-faults at the same place.
//
//   ⇒ E1 is INCOMPARABLE with EFC(PRES) and E2 sits strictly BELOW it. Same engine, same public
//     API, same class of fault; the only difference is which phase it hit.
//     THAT IS THE FINDING, and it is a REQUIREMENT ON THE STATIC TIER: whatever
//     `_od`/`_odp` grow as an error path, the choice among C1/C2/C3 must be
//     ONE choice, visible in the emitted source.
//
// ⚠ THE INTERPRETER IS NOT BEING REPAIRED. It is being deleted; patching it
// would hide the finding. The assertions below pin BOTH answers — the closed
// form AND today's engine — so a future change to either one is loud.
//
// ── THE THREE WITNESSES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  (1) HAND-DERIVED CONSTANT — every expected row set is spelled out in the
//      comment above its cell and built as literals.
//  (2) INDEPENDENT ORACLE, DIFFERENT SHAPE — `efc`: depth-first enumeration of
//      simple paths from every start node, carrying a checked running sum. No
//      fixpoint, no delta, no shadow set, no phases, no compact. It computes
//      EFC directly from the definition above.
//  (3) THE ENGINE — `IncrRec`. ⚠ SLOT (3) IS THE SLOT THE STATIC TIER TAKES:
//      when `_od`/`_odp` grow an error path, they are added as witness (3b) and
//      checked against (1) and (2) by the same helpers. Witness (3) is NOT an
//      oracle for (3b): they run the same three-phase algorithm.
//
// ── WHY SIMPLE PATHS SUFFICE, DERIVED ─────────────────────────────────────
// A derivation of `path(a,b,s)` is a walk a→…→b whose prefix sums are all
// representable and whose total is s. Both graphs below are ACYCLIC, so every
// walk IS a simple path and the enumeration is exhaustive. (Stated because it
// would be false on a cyclic graph, where the `s` column makes the relation
// infinite — which is itself why the graphs are DAGs.)
//
// ── HOW MUCH WORK THE ENGINE DOES BEFORE EACH ASSERTION ───────────────────
// ⚠ THE FAULT IS PLACED LATE, ON PURPOSE. A previous round wrote a
// correct-looking "the handle is untouched after a refusal" assertion that
// passed under its own control because the retracted row matched no partner and
// NOTHING HAD BEEN FOLDED — the cheapest possible refusal proves nothing.
// Here, before E1's fault fires, phase 1a has already seeded the over-delete set
// and phase 1b has propagated it through THREE hops (0→1 ⇒ (0,2) ⇒ (0,3)); the
// fault is the FOURTH extension. Before E2's fault fires, phase 1 has run to its
// fixpoint, phase 2 has REBUILT the whole relation and its hash index, and phase
// 3 has re-run every base body and entered its fixpoint loop. Neither cell can
// pass by an engine that refused early and did nothing.
//
// EXIT CODES: 90-96 setup; 10-19 the ORACLE disagrees with the hand constant (a
// bug in this file, reported apart from an engine bug); 20-29 cell E1; 30-39
// cell E2; 40-49 cell E3. All ≤ 125.
//
// exit 0 = the closed forms hold AND the engine diverges from them in exactly
//          the three ways described above.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.arith;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema Edge : code(0x0E5D_0000_0000_0001) {
    src: i64 = 0,
    dst: i64 = 1,
    w:   i64 = 2,
}
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ Edge };

fn i64_max() -> i64 { return 9223372036854775807i64; }
// 2^62. Two of these in one prefix sum overflow i64; one does not. Every weight
// below is either 1 or exactly this, so which derivations fault is decidable by
// counting big edges, and the comments do exactly that.
fn big() -> i64 { return 4611686018427387904i64; }
fn nmax() -> i64 { return 8i64; }

fn mk_edge(d: &Writ, s: i64, t: i64, w: i64) -> WAny {
    let e: Edge = d.make::<Edge>();
    e.src = s; e.dst = t; e.w = w;
    return WAny::ref_to((e.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn build_src(d: &Writ, hs: &Vec<WAny>, wts: &Vec<i64>) -> WAny {
    let arr: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(64i64);
    let n: i64 = hs.len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < n {
        if wts.get(i) > 0i64 { arr.push(hs.get(i)); }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return WAny::ref_to(arr as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8);
}

fn src_apply(hs: &Vec<WAny>, wts: &mut Vec<i64>, batch: &mut ZBatch, idx: i64, raw: i64) {
    let old: i64 = wts.get(idx);
    let mut neww: i64 = old + raw;
    if neww < 0i64 { neww = 0i64; }
    let eff: i64 = neww - old;
    wts.set(idx, neww);
    if eff != 0i64 { batch.push(hs.get(idx), eff); }
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// A ROW SET — three parallel columns, set semantics, no dedup machinery shared
// with anything. `ts_add` refuses a duplicate by reporting it (see `dup`),
// because a duplicate in the ENGINE's read-back is itself a defect and must not
// be able to hide inside a "contains" comparison.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
struct TriSet { a: Vec<i64>, b: Vec<i64>, s: Vec<i64>, dup: bool, nul: bool }

fn ts_new() -> TriSet {
    return TriSet { a: vec_new::<i64>(), b: vec_new::<i64>(), s: vec_new::<i64>(),
                    dup: false, nul: false };
}
fn ts_has(r: &TriSet, a: i64, b: i64, s: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < r.a.len() {
        if r.a.get(i) == a && r.b.get(i) == b && r.s.get(i) == s { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}
fn ts_add(r: &mut TriSet, a: i64, b: i64, s: i64) {
    if ts_has(r, a, b, s) { r.dup = true; return; }
    r.a.push(a); r.b.push(b); r.s.push(s);
}
fn ts_len(r: &TriSet) -> i64 { return r.a.len(); }
// every row of `x` is in `y`
fn ts_subset(x: &TriSet, y: &TriSet) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < x.a.len() {
        if !ts_has(y, x.a.get(i), x.b.get(i), x.s.get(i)) { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return true;
}
fn ts_eq(x: &TriSet, y: &TriSet) -> bool {
    if ts_len(x) != ts_len(y) { return false; }
    return ts_subset(x, y) && ts_subset(y, x);
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WITNESS (2) — EFC BY SIMPLE-PATH ENUMERATION WITH CHECKED PREFIX SUMS.
// All weights here are ≥ 0, so the overflow predicate is `w > MAX - acc`; that
// is the definition of representability, not a copy of `checked_add`'s branch.
// A path whose prefix overflows contributes NOTHING and is not extended — which
// is exactly what `eval_sexpr`'s `RtVal::Error` means for the rule, derived from
// the rule and not read off the evaluator.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn efc_walk(esrc: &Vec<i64>, edst: &Vec<i64>, ew: &Vec<i64>, pres: &Vec<i64>,
            start: i64, at: i64, acc: i64, seen: &mut Vec<i64>, out: &mut TriSet) {
    let ne: i64 = esrc.len();
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < ne {
        if pres.get(i) > 0i64 && esrc.get(i) == at {
            let v: i64 = edst.get(i);
            if seen.get(v) == 0i64 {
                let w: i64 = ew.get(i);
                if w <= i64_max() - acc {          // prefix sum representable
                    let s: i64 = acc + w;
                    if !ts_has(out, start, v, s) { ts_add(out, start, v, s); }
                    seen.set(v, 1i64);
                    efc_walk(esrc, edst, ew, pres, start, v, s, seen, out);
                    seen.set(v, 0i64);
                }
            }
        }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
}

fn efc(esrc: &Vec<i64>, edst: &Vec<i64>, ew: &Vec<i64>, pres: &Vec<i64>) -> TriSet {
    let mut out: TriSet = ts_new();
    let mut st: i64 = 0i64;
    while st < nmax() {
        let mut seen: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
        let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
        while i < nmax() { seen.push(0i64); i = i + 1i64; }
        seen.set(st, 1i64);
        efc_walk(esrc, edst, ew, pres, st, st, 0i64, &mut seen, &mut out);
        st = st + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// The engine's snapshot, read back into the same shape. A NULL in the sum column
// would mean a faulting derivation was COMMITTED with a hole in it — a fourth
// residual shape — so it is latched rather than coerced.
fn eng_rows(eng: &IncrRec) -> TriSet {
    let mut out: TriSet = ts_new();
    let r: QRows = eng.snapshot();
    let n: i64 = r.row_count();
    let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
    while k < n {
        if r.is_null(k, 0i64) || r.is_null(k, 1i64) || r.is_null(k, 2i64) { out.nul = true; }
        else { ts_add(&mut out, r.get_i64(k, 0i64), r.get_i64(k, 1i64), r.get_i64(k, 2i64)); }
        k = k + 1i64;
    }
    return out;
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ⚠ CONTROLS, MEASURED 2026-08-07 on this tree. Each is a REMOVAL of a whole
// statement in `fn dred` (stdlib/mem/deem/incr_rec.logos), built with the
// archives dropped (`rm -f build/lib/logos/liblogos-*.a` + full stdlib rebuild)
// and then REVERTED. What the emitted artifact differs by is named in each case,
// and in neither case does the ground string live in the branch being changed —
// the assertions read the RELATION, and the controls change which rows reach it.
//
//  • CONTROL P1 — PHASE 1b NO LONGER ABORTS THE EPOCH. `if chk_bad(cp) {
//    self.math_err = true; return; }` (incr_rec.logos:855) → the `return` is
//    removed, so a phase-1b fault falls through into COMPACT and RE-DERIVE.
//    PREDICTED: E1 stops being the pre-retraction closure — compact runs, so
//    (0,1,1) and the other (0,·) facts leave — and E1's `ts_eq(eng, T)` reds.
//    MEASURED: exit 22.
//
//  • CONTROL P3-a — THE RE-DERIVE FIXPOINT NO LONGER STOPS AT A FAULT. `if
//    chk_bad(cp) { break; }` at the head of phase 3's `loop`
//    (incr_rec.logos:~907) removed. PREDICTED: E2's collateral loss disappears.
//    MEASURED: exit 0 — ⚠ THE PREDICTION WAS WRONG AND THE CONTROL MEASURED
//    NOTHING. The loop head was never what dropped the row; the ROUND had
//    already been discarded one level down. This is recorded rather than deleted
//    because a control that changes nothing is this repo's 9th named gate lie,
//    and the only defence is to say which side actually moved.
//
//  • CONTROL P3-b — THE FAULTED ROUND IS NO LONGER DISCARDED WHOLESALE. In
//    `fn qrel_run_body` (stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos:227) the line
//    `if chk_bad(chk) { return; }` is removed, so the round's output table is
//    pushed even though one of its cells faulted.
//    PREDICTED: (4,6, 2^62+1) is derived after all ⇒ E2 reds; and the faulting
//    candidate (3,5) arrives as a row with a NULL sum, which `eng_rows` latches.
//    MEASURED: exit 32 — the NULL latch fires first. Re-run through a DIAGNOSTIC
//    COPY with the NULL latch and the subset check relaxed, to see the row
//    itself: exit 37, i.e. the residual then EQUALS EFC(PRES) — (4,6, 2^62+1) is
//    present. So the row really is recoverable and assertion 38 is not vacuous;
//    it is ordered before the set-shape checks so a future control reds on the
//    ROW rather than on a summary.
//
// These are what make the file a spec rather than a recording: E1 is red if the
// phase-1 abort stops being all-or-nothing, E2 is red if a faulted round stops
// taking its innocent siblings with it.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
fn main() -> i32 {
    let doc: Writ = writ_new(262144i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();
    // A PLAIN (non-aggregate) linear-recursive rel — so retraction goes through
    // `fn dred`, not `fn recompute` — carrying an ARITHMETIC column, which is
    // what gives the recursive body a cell that can fault at all.
    let q: Query = match Query::compile(
        r#"rel path(a: i64, b: i64, s: i64) {
             from edges e select (e.src, e.dst, e.w);
             from path p join edges e on p.b == e.src select (p.a, e.dst, p.s + e.w);
           }
           from path r select (r.a, r.b, r.s)"#,
        &c)
    { Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 90i32; } };

    let b: i64 = big();

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // CELL E1 — A FAULT IN PHASE 1b. THE RESIDUAL IS A STRICT SUPERSET.
    //
    // edges  0→1 w1 [0] · 1→2 w1 [1] · 2→3 wB [2] · 3→4 wB [3, ABSENT at build]
    // BUILD closure T, by hand (all six walks of the chain 0→1→2→3):
    //   (0,1,1) (1,2,1) (2,3,B) (0,2,2) (1,3,B+1) (0,3,B+2)          6 rows
    //   — B+2 is representable, so the build faults nowhere.
    // EPOCH: retract 0→1 AND insert 3→4 in ONE batch (mixed ±, which is what
    //   puts a NEW overflowing derivation in front of phase 1b).
    // PRES = {1→2 w1, 2→3 wB, 3→4 wB}.
    // EFC(PRES), by hand: (1,2,1) (2,3,B) (3,4,B) (1,3,B+1)          4 rows
    //   — (2,4) needs B+B and (1,4) needs B+1+B: both overflow, both dropped.
    // WHERE THE FAULT LANDS: OD seed = base⋈DE = {(0,1,1)}; the existing-rel⋈DE
    //   term finds no row ending at 0. Phase 1b then propagates along PRES:
    //   (0,2,2), (0,3,B+2), and the FOURTH extension (0,4) = B+2+B OVERFLOWS.
    // MEASURED: the relation is T, unchanged — 6 rows, including (0,1,1) whose
    //   only support was just deleted, and NOT including (3,4,B) whose edge was
    //   just inserted and whose derivation is overflow-free. `had_error()` true,
    //   `is_converged()` TRUE. `self.ewt` already holds PRES.
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    let mut s1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut w1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut p1: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s1.push(0i64); d1.push(1i64); w1.push(1i64); p1.push(1i64);
    s1.push(1i64); d1.push(2i64); w1.push(1i64); p1.push(1i64);
    s1.push(2i64); d1.push(3i64); w1.push(b);    p1.push(1i64);
    s1.push(3i64); d1.push(4i64); w1.push(b);    p1.push(0i64);

    // WITNESS (1) — the build closure T, literally.
    let mut want_t: TriSet = ts_new();
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 1i64, 2i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 2i64, 3i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 0i64, 2i64, 2i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 1i64, 3i64, b + 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t, 0i64, 3i64, b + 2i64);
    // WITNESS (2) checked against (1) BEFORE the engine is consulted, so an
    // oracle bug can never be reported as an engine bug.
    if !ts_eq(&efc(&s1, &d1, &w1, &p1), &want_t) { return 10i32; }

    let mut hs1: Vec<WAny> = vec_new::<WAny>();
    let mut i1: i64 = 0i64;
    while i1 < s1.len() { hs1.push(mk_edge(&doc, s1.get(i1), d1.get(i1), w1.get(i1))); i1 = i1 + 1i64; }
    let mut env1: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    env1.bind_source("edges", build_src(&doc, &hs1, &p1));
    let mut eng1: IncrRec = match q.incremental_rec(&env1)
        { Result::Ok(e) => { e } Result::Err(_e2) => { return 91i32; } };
    if eng1.had_error() { return 92i32; }
    let b1: TriSet = eng_rows(&eng1);
    if b1.dup || b1.nul { return 93i32; }
    if !ts_eq(&b1, &want_t) { return 20i32; }

    let mut z1: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    src_apply(&hs1, &mut p1, &mut z1, 0i64, 0i64 - 1i64);   // retract 0→1
    src_apply(&hs1, &mut p1, &mut z1, 3i64, 1i64);          // insert 3→4
    eng1.epoch(&z1);

    // WITNESS (1) for EFC(PRES), literally, then WITNESS (2) against it.
    let mut want_efc1: TriSet = ts_new();
    ts_add(&mut want_efc1, 1i64, 2i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc1, 2i64, 3i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc1, 3i64, 4i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc1, 1i64, 3i64, b + 1i64);
    let efc1: TriSet = efc(&s1, &d1, &w1, &p1);
    if !ts_eq(&efc1, &want_efc1) { return 11i32; }

    let a1: TriSet = eng_rows(&eng1);
    if a1.dup || a1.nul { return 94i32; }
    // (i) THE FAULT IS REPORTED.
    if !eng1.had_error() { return 21i32; }
    // (ii) THE RESIDUAL IS THE PRE-RETRACTION CLOSURE, EXACTLY — the epoch was a
    //      no-op ON THE RELATION although the batch contained both a retraction
    //      and an insertion, each of which was individually applicable.
    if !ts_eq(&a1, &want_t) { return 22i32; }
    // (iii) ⚠ IT IS NOT COMPARABLE WITH EFC(PRES) IN EITHER DIRECTION, and both
    //      directions are named by a row:
    //        · (0,1,1) is IN the residual and NOT in EFC(PRES) — a fact whose
    //          only support is an edge the engine has already dropped from
    //          `self.ewt`. Over-RETENTION.
    //        · (3,4,B) is in EFC(PRES) and NOT in the residual — the insertion
    //          that shared the batch, overflow-free, simply lost. Under-DERIVATION.
    //      So the residual is not "the old answer" and not "the new answer": it
    //      is the old answer measured against a DIFFERENT edge set, which is a
    //      state no single epoch's input describes.
    if ts_subset(&a1, &efc1) { return 23i32; }
    if ts_subset(&efc1, &a1) { return 24i32; }
    if !ts_has(&a1, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64) { return 25i32; }
    if ts_has(&efc1, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64) { return 17i32; }
    if ts_has(&a1, 3i64, 4i64, b) { return 26i32; }
    if !ts_has(&efc1, 3i64, 4i64, b) { return 18i32; }
    // (v) ⚠ AND THE ENGINE SAYS IT IS EXACT. `is_converged()` is documented
    //      "true iff the maintained state is the exact least fixpoint", and the
    //      maintained state is the fixpoint of an edge set the engine no longer
    //      holds. THIS IS THE ASSERTION THAT MUST NOT BE "FIXED" AWAY QUIETLY.
    if !eng1.is_converged() { return 27i32; }

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // CELL E2 — A FAULT IN PHASE 3. THE RESIDUAL IS A STRICT SUBSET, AND WHAT
    // IT LOST IS IN A DIFFERENT CONNECTED COMPONENT.
    //
    // edges  0→1 w1 [0] · 1→2 w1 [1] · 3→4 wB [2] · 5→6 w1 [3] ·
    //        4→5 wB [4, ABSENT at build]
    // BUILD closure, by hand: (0,1,1) (1,2,1) (3,4,B) (5,6,1) (0,2,2)  5 rows
    // EPOCH: retract 1→2 (component A) AND insert 4→5 (component B), one batch.
    // PRES = {0→1 w1, 3→4 wB, 5→6 w1, 4→5 wB}.
    // EFC(PRES), by hand:
    //   base    (0,1,1) (3,4,B) (5,6,1) (4,5,B)
    //   derived (4,6,B+1)   ← 4→5→6, sum B+1, REPRESENTABLE
    //   dropped (3,5) = B+B overflows; (3,6) needs (3,5) so it cannot exist
    //                                                                  5 rows
    // WHERE THE FAULT LANDS: the OD set is confined to component A (seed
    //   {(1,2,1)}, propagation adds (0,2,2) via the existing-rel⋈DE term), so
    //   phase 1 never touches component B and does NOT fault. Phase 2 compacts.
    //   Phase 3 re-runs the base bodies (no arithmetic ⇒ no fault) and enters
    //   its fixpoint, where (3,4)⋈4→5 = B+B FAULTS.
    // MEASURED: 4 rows — (0,1,1) (3,4,B) (5,6,1) (4,5,B). The retraction WAS
    //   applied (both (1,2,1) and (0,2,2) are gone, so phases 2 and 3 really
    //   ran), `had_error()` true, `is_converged()` TRUE — and (4,6,B+1) IS
    //   MISSING, a fact with no overflowing derivation anywhere, lost because
    //   `if chk_bad(cp) { break; }` ended the fixpoint that would have derived
    //   it. THE FAULT COST A FACT IT HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH.
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    let mut s2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut d2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    let mut w2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>(); let mut p2: Vec<i64> = vec_new::<i64>();
    s2.push(0i64); d2.push(1i64); w2.push(1i64); p2.push(1i64);
    s2.push(1i64); d2.push(2i64); w2.push(1i64); p2.push(1i64);
    s2.push(3i64); d2.push(4i64); w2.push(b);    p2.push(1i64);
    s2.push(5i64); d2.push(6i64); w2.push(1i64); p2.push(1i64);
    s2.push(4i64); d2.push(5i64); w2.push(b);    p2.push(0i64);

    let mut want_t2: TriSet = ts_new();
    ts_add(&mut want_t2, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t2, 1i64, 2i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t2, 3i64, 4i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_t2, 5i64, 6i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_t2, 0i64, 2i64, 2i64);
    if !ts_eq(&efc(&s2, &d2, &w2, &p2), &want_t2) { return 12i32; }

    let mut hs2: Vec<WAny> = vec_new::<WAny>();
    let mut i2: i64 = 0i64;
    while i2 < s2.len() { hs2.push(mk_edge(&doc, s2.get(i2), d2.get(i2), w2.get(i2))); i2 = i2 + 1i64; }
    let mut env2: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    env2.bind_source("edges", build_src(&doc, &hs2, &p2));
    let mut eng2: IncrRec = match q.incremental_rec(&env2)
        { Result::Ok(e) => { e } Result::Err(_e3) => { return 95i32; } };
    if eng2.had_error() { return 96i32; }
    let b2: TriSet = eng_rows(&eng2);
    if b2.dup || b2.nul { return 30i32; }
    if !ts_eq(&b2, &want_t2) { return 31i32; }

    let mut z2: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    src_apply(&hs2, &mut p2, &mut z2, 1i64, 0i64 - 1i64);   // retract 1→2
    src_apply(&hs2, &mut p2, &mut z2, 4i64, 1i64);          // insert 4→5
    eng2.epoch(&z2);

    let mut want_efc2: TriSet = ts_new();
    ts_add(&mut want_efc2, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc2, 3i64, 4i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc2, 5i64, 6i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc2, 4i64, 5i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc2, 4i64, 6i64, b + 1i64);
    let efc2: TriSet = efc(&s2, &d2, &w2, &p2);
    if !ts_eq(&efc2, &want_efc2) { return 13i32; }

    let a2: TriSet = eng_rows(&eng2);
    if a2.dup || a2.nul { return 32i32; }
    if !eng2.had_error() { return 33i32; }
    // (i) THE RETRACTION REALLY WAS APPLIED — phases 2 and 3 ran. This is what
    //     separates E2 from E1 and it is asserted BY NAME, not by a count.
    if ts_has(&a2, 1i64, 2i64, 1i64) { return 34i32; }
    if ts_has(&a2, 0i64, 2i64, 2i64) { return 35i32; }
    // (ii) ⚠ THE COLLATERAL LOSS, NAMED — CHECKED FIRST so that a control which
    //      restores the row reds on THE ROW and not on a set-shape summary.
    //      (4,6, 2^62+1) has exactly one derivation, 4→5→6, whose prefix sums are
    //      B and B+1 — neither overflows. It is missing anyway.
    //      WHY, named: `fn qrel_run_body` (stdlib/mem/deem/query.logos:227) is
    //      ALL-OR-NOTHING — `let out = exec_root(...); if chk_bad(chk) { return; }`
    //      discards the WHOLE round's output table when any single projection
    //      cell faulted, so the correct (4,5)⋈5→6 candidate produced in the same
    //      round as the faulting (3,4)⋈4→5 one is thrown away with it, and phase
    //      3's loop then sees an empty delta and exits. THIS ROW IS THE WHOLE
    //      POINT OF THE CELL: a fault costs facts it has nothing to do with, in a
    //      DIFFERENT CONNECTED COMPONENT of the graph.
    if ts_has(&a2, 4i64, 6i64, b + 1i64) { return 38i32; }
    if !ts_has(&efc2, 4i64, 6i64, b + 1i64) { return 14i32; }
    // (iii) AND THE RESIDUAL IS A STRICT SUBSET OF EFC(PRES) — the OPPOSITE side
    //      of the answer from E1, out of the same class of fault.
    if !ts_subset(&a2, &efc2) { return 36i32; }
    if ts_eq(&a2, &efc2) { return 37i32; }
    // (iv) and the engine calls this converged, too.
    if !eng2.is_converged() { return 39i32; }

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // CELL E3 — THE FAULTED STATE IS ABSORBING: RETRACTING THE OFFENDING EDGE
    // DOES NOT CLEAR IT.
    //
    // Continue from E2 and retract 4→5 — the very edge whose weight made the
    // sum overflow. Under any of C1/C2/C3 the engine should now be able to
    // return to a consistent state: PRES' = {0→1 w1, 3→4 wB, 5→6 w1} and
    // EFC(PRES') = (0,1,1) (3,4,B) (5,6,1)  — 3 rows, no arithmetic anywhere
    // near an overflow.
    // MEASURED: NOTHING CHANGES. The relation is still E2's four rows, still
    //   containing (4,5,B) whose supporting edge has just been removed.
    // WHY, named: phase 1a's existing-rel⋈DE term joins the surviving row
    //   (3,4,B) with the deleted edge 4→5 and computes B+B, which faults inside
    //   PHASE 1a; `chk_bad` then makes phase 1b's loop break on its first
    //   iteration and :855 returns before COMPACT. Every subsequent retraction
    //   that reaches that row faults at the same place, so the inconsistency is
    //   a FIXED POINT of the API.
    // ⇒ RECORDED AS A REQUIREMENT ON THE STATIC TIER: an error path that leaves
    //   the relation in a state which re-faults on the next retraction is not a
    //   partially-applied retraction, it is a WEDGE. Whichever of C1/C2/C3 the
    //   emitted code picks, it must be reachable-out-of.
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    let mut z3: ZBatch = ZBatch::new();
    src_apply(&hs2, &mut p2, &mut z3, 4i64, 0i64 - 1i64);   // retract 4→5
    eng2.epoch(&z3);

    let mut want_efc3: TriSet = ts_new();
    ts_add(&mut want_efc3, 0i64, 1i64, 1i64);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc3, 3i64, 4i64, b);
    ts_add(&mut want_efc3, 5i64, 6i64, 1i64);
    let efc3: TriSet = efc(&s2, &d2, &w2, &p2);
    if !ts_eq(&efc3, &want_efc3) { return 15i32; }

    let a3: TriSet = eng_rows(&eng2);
    if a3.dup || a3.nul { return 40i32; }
    // (i) the state did not move at all.
    if !ts_eq(&a3, &a2) { return 41i32; }
    // (ii) and it still holds a fact whose supporting edge is gone.
    if !ts_has(&a3, 4i64, 5i64, b) { return 42i32; }
    // (iii) which is exactly the row EFC(PRES') does not contain.
    if ts_has(&efc3, 4i64, 5i64, b) { return 16i32; }
    // (iv) the error stays latched and the engine still claims convergence.
    if !eng2.had_error() { return 43i32; }
    if !eng2.is_converged() { return 44i32; }

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // BLOCKERS — THE PART OF REGION 5 NO FIXTURE CAN REACH, IN THE
    // NAMED-FUNCTIONS / NAMED-LOCALS SHAPE. THIS RECORD *IS* THE DELIVERABLE
    // FOR THEM.
    //
    // BLOCKER R5-a — THE THIRD ABORT EXIT IS UNREACHABLE FROM A FIXTURE.
    //   `fn dred` (incr_rec.logos:776) has a third early return, at :797:
    //       ir_check(q, &self.env, &mut qp, &mut chk);
    //       if chk.failed { return; }
    //   It returns WITHOUT setting `self.math_err`, so a caller sees
    //   `had_error() == false` over a relation that is the PRE-retraction
    //   closure while `self.ewt` already holds PRES — a SILENT no-op retraction,
    //   strictly worse than E1 because nothing at all reports it.
    //   WHY NO FIXTURE REACHES IT: `ir_check` is a static check of the compiled
    //   plan against the bound env, and `fn recompute` (:413) runs the SAME
    //   check at :426 during `Query::incremental_rec`. A plan that fails it
    //   never produces an engine — `incremental_rec` returns `Err` first — so
    //   there is no input that passes the build check and fails the identical
    //   dred check. Reaching it needs an env whose SHAPE changes between epochs,
    //   which `bind_source` does not offer.
    //   THE REQUIREMENT IT RECORDS: the static tier must not have an abort that
    //   is invisible to its error accessor. E1 and E2 both set the flag; this
    //   one does not, and it is the same function.
    //
    // BLOCKER R5-b — THE FAULTING ROW ITSELF IS NOT OBSERVABLE.
    //   `had_error()` (incr_rec.logos:922) is a latched BOOLEAN. `Chk.msg`
    //   (check.logos:40) holds the diagnostic text but `fn dred` drops its local
    //   `chk` at return and nothing copies the message onto the engine, so a
    //   caller cannot learn WHICH derivation faulted or HOW MANY did. Every
    //   assertion above therefore infers the fault's position from the RESIDUAL
    //   SHAPE — superset vs subset — which is why E1 and E2 have to be two
    //   different graphs instead of one graph and one accessor.
    //   THE REQUIREMENT: an emitted error path should carry the faulting row,
    //   not a bit.
    //
    // BLOCKER R5-c — "PARTIALLY COMPACTED" IS NOT AMONG THE RESIDUALS, AND THAT
    //   IS NOT PROVEN, ONLY UNOBSERVED. Phase 2 (incr_rec.logos:857-890) builds
    //   `new_rows`/`new_rhm` fresh and only then assigns `self.rows`/`self.rhm`,
    //   so it is atomic BY CONSTRUCTION — reading the code, not measuring it. No
    //   fixture can fault INSIDE phase 2, because phase 2 evaluates no
    //   expression: it copies rows and hashes them. The record exists so a
    //   static tier that compacts IN PLACE is understood to be introducing a
    //   fourth residual that the interpreter never had.
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    return 0i32;
}
